n PROGRESS.md and the report and do the smallest sound thing.   - `CLAUDE.md`: the owner's rules on compute (only unsolved problems;     never the Python pathwidth solver; never `solutions_dir=None`), what is     proved and what is measured, \"Lessons that cost sessions\".   - The Lean tools: `lean/MOSPFormalization/TreeBound.lean` (its header     lists `pathwidth_le_pathwidth_erase_add_one`,     `pathwidth_le_pathwidth_induce_add_card_compl`,     `pathwidth_induce_union_le_max`, `pathwidth_induce_le_of_forall_connected`,     `PathDecomposition.extend`, `cat`, `glue`, `reachSet`,     `tree_pathwidth_le_log`), `Minor.lean` (`isolate`, `pathwidth_mono`,     `pathwidth_eq_of_iso`), `Pathwidth.lean`, `PathDecomposition.lean`,     `EmptyPair.lean`, `ForestCover.lean`; the named-Prop pattern in     `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/EdgeSearch.lean` (`EdgeSearchMonotonicity`,     a `def … : Prop`, never asserted, with the theorems that need it taking     it as a hypothesis `(h : EdgeSearchMonotonicity G)`); `paper1/axiom_check.lean`.   - The source, once item 01 has fetched it:     `reports/scratch/edwards_mcdermid/em2015.pdf` and `em2015.txt`     (Sections 1, 3, 5 and the Appendix); the held     `literature/2006-Fomin-Hoie-Pathwidth-Cubic-Graphs-Exact-Algorithms-IPL.pdf`. - **The object.** `g : ℕ → ℚ` with `g 0 = g 1 = g 2 = 0`, `g 3 = 1/6`,   `n g n = (n − 2) g (n − 1) + g (n − 2) + 1` for `n ≥ 4`; `g' n = g n − g (n − 1)`   (so `g' d = 0` for `d ≤ 2`); `f G = Σ_v g (G.degree v)`;   `fminus G = f G − Σ_{components C} g' (minDeg C)`. The series-parallel   operations act on `SimpleGraph V` with removed vertices isolated;   `SPReduces` is their reflexive–transitive closure; a graph is *reduced*   when every degree is 0 or ≥ 3, *basic* when every component of its   support is cubic or becomes `⊥` by reduction after one vertex is removed.   The two named hypotheses are `CubicPathwidthBound τ` (every connected   cubic graph on `N` vertices has `pw ≤ N/6 − 1/6 + τ N`) and   `ReductionPathwidthBound ρ` (`SPReduces G H → pw G ≤ pw H + ρ |V|`);   the theorem is `pw G ≤ fminus G + τ n + 2 ρ n + 1` for graphs with every   degree 0 or ≥ 3, and `pw G ≤ Σ_v g (degree_{r G} v) + τ n + 3 ρ n + 1`   for every `G`. Say in every statement which budget it uses; the paper's   `τ(n) + O(log n)` is this with `ρ = O(log n)`, and the paper gives no   constants (plan §0.3). - **Nothing is solved twice.** Every pathwidth the loop uses is on record:   the pilot (`reports/scratch/gnp_pilot/results.csv`: 6,542 proved rows,   `status ∈ {refutation, bound, minor}`; graphs regenerate as   `networkx.gnp_random_graph(n, c/n, seed=seed)`, the `layout` column is a   witness; the 173 `upper` rows are unproved and only their `lower` column   may be used), the dataset (`paper1/data/dataset/pathwidth_dataset.jsonl.gz`,   16,636 certified classes with `edges`, `width`, `provenance`), the   ensembles (`learning/data/ensemble/results*.csv`, regenerate each instance   from its seed with `learning.ensemble.generate(Cell.parse(...), index)`,   MOSP graph by `customer_inter.customer_graph.build_customer_graph`,   `pw = optimum − 1`). New exact solves are allowed only where an item says   so: every graph to 9 vertices from `nauty-geng` and every cubic graph to   16 vertices (`nauty-geng -d3 -D3`), by the subset DP   (`learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute`, `n ≤ 16`, or   `fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`, `n ≤ 18`); never   the Python pathwidth solver; never `solutions_dir=None`; never `solutions/`. - **State before you prove, and check before you state.** Every statement   that will be called a lemma or theorem — on paper or in Lean — is first   checked by brute force on small instances (every graph to 9 vertices, as   `reports/scratch/empty_pair/check.py` does), and the check's script and   log are kept under `reports/scratch/edwards_mcdermid/<item>/`. Two earlier   `sorry`s in this development hid false statements, not hard ones; this   loop adds none, and a cited result is a named `Prop`, never a `sorry` or   an `axiom`. - **A measurement is not a theorem; a budget the record needs is not the   paper's budget.** Every number carries its size range and its regenerate   command. Where the lower-order term is measured (the smallest constant   the record requires), say \"measured on … to `n = …`\", never \"the   constant\". Every item ends with \"what this does not claim\". Say \"we found   no prior statement\", never \"first\". This is a formalisation of a   published theorem (Edwards & McDermid 2015, Lemma 5.1 and Corollary 5.2),   conditional on two cited results (Fomin & Høie 2006 Theorem 5; Kneis et   al. 2009): say so plainly, every time. - **The planner's numbers in the plan** (the Poisson values, `g` table,   Fomin–Høie's `n_ε`, the `5/6` floor) are ten-second checks to be replaced   by item 01's, never quoted as results.  ## Compute, and the machine - 32 cores, shared with the owner's other projects; the load is often above   24 from work that is not ours. Every process under `nice -n 19`; at most   8 workers, 4 if the one-minute load in `uptime` is above 24. Check   `uptime` before anything parallel. Find processes by PID, never by   command-line text; never a bare `pkill -f <word>`. - The owner's `reports/scratch/dataset_open/run_unlimited.py` (24 workers at   nice 19) may be running or may be restarted at any time; it must not be   disturbed, and its idle cores are the owner's, not ours. - Budgets per item are in `iterations.md`; the loop's total is about 4   core-hours. Long jobs run in the foreground with bounded polls; never end   a turn on a background wait. - Only one `lake build` at a time (the loop is sequential, so this holds by   construction; do not start a second build in the background). - The manuscript (item 01) goes to `reports/scratch/edwards_mcdermid/`, not   `literature/`; nothing is executed from a download; text is extracted with   `pdftotext`; Python that reads a download runs with `-I`.  ## Current Focus Item 01 fetches and verifies. Item 02 writes the exact statements and checks every intermediate lemma on every graph to 9 vertices. Items 03–09 are Lean, in order of reach: the weights (03), the independent cut lemma (04), the potential's deletion accounting (05), the reduction system with the commutation lemma (06), the step inequality (07), Theorem 3.2 (08), the pathwidth assembly under the named hypotheses with the axiom check (09). Item 10 writes; 11 is the reserve.  ## Target Problems (in order) See `iterations.md`. Work on exactly ONE unchecked item per iteration; the item's full text is in the prompt and in the file. An item that depends on a blocked (`- [!]`) item states the named `Prop` that stands in for the missing result and proceeds with what is independent.  ## Acceptance Criteria (per item) - [ ] The deliverable exists where the item says, states its size range,       gives its regenerate command, has a kill verdict (met / not met /       none), and says what it does not claim. - [ ] New code has tests under `tests/`; new Lean has no `sorry` and no       `axiom`, is imported by `lean/MOSPFormalization.lean`, and every new       theorem has a `#print axioms` line in `paper1/axiom_check.lean`. - [ ] Every new Lean statement was checked by brute force first and the       check's log is under `reports/scratch/edwards_mcdermid/`. - [ ] `python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0012/gate.py` passes (lake build, imports,       sorry and axiom count at zero, MOSP pytest about 3 min,       pathwidth_solver pytest about 6 min, `make -C paper1/latex check`).       Run `make -C paper1/latex` before the gate if the PDF is stale. - [ ] A section appended to `reports/edwards_mcdermid.md` (create it in       item 01), and an entry in PROGRESS.md: what was done, the numbers,       the kill verdict, what is left. - [ ] The item is marked `- [x]`, or `- [!]` with the reason in PROGRESS.md.  ## Completion Conditions An item is DONE when solved, or blocked with t